1
00:00:00,690 --> 00:00:03,610
The next section that we want to discuss

2
00:00:03,610 --> 00:00:06,190
concerns macrocytic anemia.

3
00:00:06,190 --> 00:00:09,190
Now in macrocytic anemia, the idea is that

4
00:00:09,190 --> 00:00:11,510
the patient develops an anemia, however,

5
00:00:11,510 --> 00:00:15,130
the red blood cells are relatively bigger than normal.

6
00:00:15,130 --> 00:00:19,110
Recall that the normal MCV is about 80 to 100,

7
00:00:19,110 --> 00:00:23,330
and in a macrocytic anemia, the MCV is greater than 100.

8
00:00:23,330 --> 00:00:26,710
We can ask ourselves, well, why would the MCV be big?

9
00:00:26,710 --> 00:00:30,490
Or in other words, what's driving the cells to be large?

10
00:00:30,490 --> 00:00:32,890
And the answer goes back to the paradigm

11
00:00:32,890 --> 00:00:35,110
that we've already established in our discussion

12
00:00:35,110 --> 00:00:36,650
of microcytic anemia.

13
00:00:36,650 --> 00:00:38,970
Remember that I told you that in microcytic anemia,

14
00:00:38,970 --> 00:00:43,890
the idea was that we started with a red blood cell precursor

15
00:00:43,890 --> 00:00:46,770
in the bone marrow, and that that red blood cell precursor

16
00:00:46,770 --> 00:00:50,670
in the bone marrow divides, divides, and divides,

17
00:00:50,670 --> 00:00:53,350
and divides several times until it eventually

18
00:00:53,350 --> 00:00:55,410
produces mature red blood cells.

19
00:00:55,410 --> 00:00:57,650
Recall that I said that the red blood cell precursor

20
00:00:57,650 --> 00:01:00,490
is a relatively large cell, and that each

21
00:01:00,490 --> 00:01:03,210
time it divides, it becomes slightly smaller.

22
00:01:03,210 --> 00:01:07,450
Now in a microcytic anemia, it's as if an extra division

23
00:01:07,450 --> 00:01:10,950
occurs, making the cells just a little bit smaller than usual.

24
00:01:10,950 --> 00:01:14,870
Well, in a macrocytic anemia, it's as if one less division

25
00:01:14,870 --> 00:01:18,430
occurs, and when one less division occurs, the cells are going

26
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:21,830
to be larger than normal, and hence the MCV will be greater

27
00:01:21,830 --> 00:01:23,110
than 100.

28
00:01:23,110 --> 00:01:27,390
We can then ask the question, why would one less division occur?

29
00:01:27,390 --> 00:01:31,390
And the answer here is that in a macrocytic anemia, there is usually

30
00:01:31,390 --> 00:01:34,990
an upset in the production of DNA precursors.

31
00:01:34,990 --> 00:01:37,850
Now remember that every time a cell divides, it requires DNA

32
00:01:37,850 --> 00:01:41,950
precursors because each division requires a copying of the nucleus.

33
00:01:41,950 --> 00:01:46,650
And so if there's a disruption in DNA precursors, the cell won't be

34
00:01:46,650 --> 00:01:50,650
able to divide because it doesn't have the necessary precursors to

35
00:01:50,650 --> 00:01:52,030
copy the nucleus.

36
00:01:52,030 --> 00:01:57,250
One less division will occur, resulting in a macrocytic anemia.

37
00:01:57,250 --> 00:02:01,330
Now this then raises the question of what are the major causes of

38
00:02:01,330 --> 00:02:02,770
macrocytic anemia.

39
00:02:02,770 --> 00:02:06,030
And of course, the major causes of macrocytic anemia will be those that

40
00:02:06,030 --> 00:02:10,290
are related to the production of DNA precursors.

41
00:02:10,290 --> 00:02:16,130
And so the two most common causes are folate deficiency and vitamin B12

42
00:02:16,130 --> 00:02:17,370
deficiency.

43
00:02:17,370 --> 00:02:21,750
Remember from biochemistry that both folate and vitamin B12 are necessary

44
00:02:21,750 --> 00:02:24,630
for the synthesis of DNA precursor molecules.

45
00:02:24,630 --> 00:02:28,210
And if folate or vitamin B12 is deficient, we won't be able to generate

46
00:02:28,210 --> 00:02:30,750
appropriate DNA precursor molecules.

47
00:02:30,750 --> 00:02:35,290
Decrease in DNA precursor molecules is going to lead to an inability of the

48
00:02:35,290 --> 00:02:41,070
cell to divide one extra time, which is then going to lead to a large red blood

49
00:02:41,070 --> 00:02:44,790
cell or a macrocytic red blood cell.

50
00:02:44,790 --> 00:02:50,670
Here we can take a step back and remind ourselves of a little bit of biochemistry.

51
00:02:50,670 --> 00:02:57,770
Remember that folate, when it comes into the body, comes into the body as tetrahydrofolate,

52
00:02:57,770 --> 00:03:02,290
and it is quickly methylated, and I'll just abbreviate this with an M here.

53
00:03:02,290 --> 00:03:06,910
Again, when folate comes into the body as tetrahydrofolate, it's quickly methylated.

54
00:03:06,910 --> 00:03:18,930
Now, in order for tetrahydrofolate to go and to participate in the synthesis of DNA precursors,

55
00:03:18,930 --> 00:03:21,210
it needs to lose this methyl group.

56
00:03:21,210 --> 00:03:28,670
And so very quickly, folate will basically convince vitamin B12 to take the methyl group.

57
00:03:28,670 --> 00:03:34,370
And so vitamin B12 will actually take this methyl group, tetrahydrofolate will lose the methyl

58
00:03:34,370 --> 00:03:40,390
group, allowing tetrahydrofolate to participate in DNA precursor synthesis.

59
00:03:40,390 --> 00:03:45,610
Now, once vitamin B12 takes the methyl group, it's as if vitamin B12 recognizes, hey, this

60
00:03:45,610 --> 00:03:49,750
methyl group is not cool, I need to get rid of it, and it very quickly passes it on to

61
00:03:49,750 --> 00:03:51,170
another molecule.

62
00:03:51,170 --> 00:03:55,210
And that molecule that it passes it on to is homocysteine.

63
00:03:55,210 --> 00:04:01,870
Now, homocysteine accepts the methyl group, and when homocysteine accepts the methyl group,

64
00:04:01,870 --> 00:04:06,300
it becomes methionine.

65
00:04:06,300 --> 00:04:11,720
And of course, you're well aware from biochemistry that methionine is important in the transfer

66
00:04:11,720 --> 00:04:16,100
of methyl groups to various other important molecules, but we don't have time to discuss

67
00:04:16,100 --> 00:04:17,100
that here.

68
00:04:17,100 --> 00:04:20,160
That's more of a biochemistry notion.

69
00:04:20,160 --> 00:04:25,880
So again, general principle, tetrahydrofolate needs to lose the methyl group to participate

70
00:04:25,880 --> 00:04:28,020
in DNA precursor synthesis.

71
00:04:28,020 --> 00:04:32,780
It hands the methyl group to vitamin B12, vitamin B12 then hands it to homocysteine.

72
00:04:32,780 --> 00:04:38,780
Now, imagine if there is a folate deficiency, if folate is knocked out, then obviously DNA

73
00:04:38,780 --> 00:04:40,460
precursors won't be made.

74
00:04:40,460 --> 00:04:47,000
And also, if vitamin B12 is missing, tetrahydrofolate won't be able to pass this methyl group to vitamin

75
00:04:47,000 --> 00:04:53,120
B12, and hence it will also not be able to participate in DNA precursor synthesis.

76
00:04:53,120 --> 00:04:59,360
And once you understand that, it's now easy to understand why I said that anemia, a macrocytic

77
00:04:59,360 --> 00:05:04,100
anemia, is most commonly due to folate or vitamin B12 deficiency.

78
00:05:04,100 --> 00:05:09,140
Now it's worth taking a moment here to just understand a little bit of terminology.

79
00:05:09,140 --> 00:05:15,760
And that is, when we have a folate or B12 deficiency, then the term that we apply to that anemia is

80
00:05:15,760 --> 00:05:18,700
called a megaloblastic anemia.

81
00:05:18,700 --> 00:05:23,120
And it's called a megaloblastic anemia because folate and vitamin B12 are obviously important

82
00:05:23,120 --> 00:05:26,680
for DNA, for synthesis of DNA precursors.

83
00:05:26,680 --> 00:05:33,480
And if we can't produce DNA precursors appropriately, not only will the red blood cells be affected,

84
00:05:33,480 --> 00:05:38,360
but other cells in the body that are rapidly dividing will also be affected.

85
00:05:38,360 --> 00:05:44,960
And so those cells can become big, i.e., the cells that are rapidly dividing can become big,

86
00:05:44,960 --> 00:05:49,520
and so it's called a megaloblastic anemia.

87
00:05:49,520 --> 00:05:51,600
So that's an important distinction in terminology.

88
00:05:51,600 --> 00:05:57,160
There are some macrocytic anemias that are not due to folate or vitamin B12 deficiency,

89
00:05:57,160 --> 00:05:59,660
and we'll briefly touch on those a little bit later.

90
00:05:59,660 --> 00:06:03,780
So now I think with that background, we can walk through the slides.

91
00:06:03,780 --> 00:06:08,480
So here I've told you in your text that folate and vitamin B12 are necessary for synthesis

92
00:06:08,480 --> 00:06:10,280
of DNA precursors.

93
00:06:10,280 --> 00:06:14,700
Recall that folate, when it comes into the body as tetrahydrofolate, it gets methylated.

94
00:06:14,700 --> 00:06:18,920
The methyl group must be given to vitamin B12 before tetrahydrofolate can participate

95
00:06:18,920 --> 00:06:21,220
in the synthesis of DNA precursors.

96
00:06:21,220 --> 00:06:28,340
So therefore, both folate and vitamin B12 are necessary for this synthesis of DNA precursors.

97
00:06:28,340 --> 00:06:33,960
A lack of folate or a lack of vitamin B12 is obviously going to impair the synthesis of

98
00:06:33,960 --> 00:06:38,120
DNA precursors, and then that will result in certain changes.

99
00:06:38,120 --> 00:06:45,400
We'll get a megaloblastic anemia with macrocytic red blood cells with an MCV greater than 100.

100
00:06:45,400 --> 00:06:52,440
Also characteristic of vitamin B12 or folate deficiency will be hypersegmented neutrophils.

101
00:06:52,440 --> 00:06:56,780
Hypersegmented neutrophils refer to neutrophils that have greater than five lobes.

102
00:06:56,780 --> 00:07:02,560
Now normally the nucleus of the neutrophil has anywhere from three to five lobes and greater

103
00:07:02,560 --> 00:07:08,340
than five lobes indicates that we're dealing with a hypersegmented neutrophil.

104
00:07:08,340 --> 00:07:13,360
Another change that we're going to see if folate or B12 is deficient is megaloblastic change

105
00:07:13,360 --> 00:07:15,400
in all rapidly dividing cells.

106
00:07:15,400 --> 00:07:20,620
So for example, if we were to look at the gut, we would see enlargement of the epithelial

107
00:07:20,620 --> 00:07:23,180
cells within the gut.

108
00:07:23,180 --> 00:07:28,920
Here's a picture of a hypersegmented neutrophil, and basically the notion here is that this

109
00:07:28,920 --> 00:07:37,020
neutrophil has one, two, three, four, five, six, seven lobes, and again anything greater

110
00:07:37,020 --> 00:07:39,420
than five is abnormal.

111
00:07:39,420 --> 00:07:43,920
In addition, you'll notice that most of these red blood cells are relatively normal in size,

112
00:07:43,920 --> 00:07:48,800
but you can see that there's a large red blood cell here, and this would be an example of

113
00:07:48,800 --> 00:07:54,460
a macroovallocyte, which is again a large red blood cell that would have an MCV greater

114
00:07:54,460 --> 00:07:59,360
than 100, but tends to have a little bit of an oval shape to it.

115
00:07:59,360 --> 00:08:05,140
These changes, both of these, the macrocytic cell along with the hypersegmented neutrophil,

116
00:08:05,140 --> 00:08:11,720
are classic findings in a megaloblastic anemia, which would be due to folate or vitamin B12

117
00:08:11,720 --> 00:08:12,880
deficiency.

118
00:08:12,880 --> 00:08:18,820
If you have a macrocytic anemia due to other than folate or B12 deficiency, then we would

119
00:08:18,820 --> 00:08:21,200
just simply call that a macrocytic anemia.

120
00:08:21,200 --> 00:08:23,960
We would not call that a megaloblastic anemia.

121
00:08:23,960 --> 00:08:28,980
And so a few other causes of macrocytic anemia to be familiar with include alcoholism, this

122
00:08:28,980 --> 00:08:34,300
gives you a macrocytic anemia, liver disease can cause a macrocytic anemia, and there are certain

123
00:08:34,300 --> 00:08:38,560
drugs, for example, 5-FU, that can cause a macrocytic anemia.

124
00:08:38,560 --> 00:08:43,680
In the case of macrocytic anemia, we would see large red blood cells with an MCV greater

125
00:08:43,680 --> 00:08:48,780
than 100, however, we would not see hypersegmented neutrophils, and we would not see megaloblastic

126
00:08:48,780 --> 00:08:52,440
change in the other rapidly dividing cells.

127
00:08:52,440 --> 00:08:58,060
That's the distinction between megaloblastic anemia and a macrocytic anemia.

128
00:08:58,060 --> 00:09:03,040
We're going to focus, obviously, on the two major causes of megaloblastic anemia.

129
00:09:03,040 --> 00:09:05,220
And the first is folate.

130
00:09:05,220 --> 00:09:10,300
Folate is the most common cause of a macrocytic or megaloblastic anemia, and so we want to

131
00:09:10,300 --> 00:09:14,280
understand a little bit about folate, in particular its biochemistry.

132
00:09:14,280 --> 00:09:19,680
Recall that folate is obtained from green vegetables and fruits, i.e., it's obtained from the diet,

133
00:09:19,680 --> 00:09:21,940
and it's absorbed in the jejunum.

134
00:09:21,940 --> 00:09:27,560
It is important for the purposes of examinations to know where folate is absorbed.

135
00:09:27,560 --> 00:09:34,020
Folate deficiency usually develops within months, and that's because the body stores are minimal.

136
00:09:34,020 --> 00:09:38,240
There is not much folate that is stored within the body.

137
00:09:38,240 --> 00:09:40,780
What are some causes of folate deficiency?

138
00:09:40,780 --> 00:09:41,740
Poor diet.

139
00:09:41,740 --> 00:09:46,320
Again, we have limited stores, and folate is obtained from the diet, so individuals with

140
00:09:46,320 --> 00:09:50,440
a poor diet will have an increased risk of developing folate deficiency.

141
00:09:50,440 --> 00:09:56,340
The classic examples are alcoholics, who tend to have a poor diet, and the elderly.

142
00:09:56,340 --> 00:10:01,500
Another way by which folate deficiency can arise is by increased demand.

143
00:10:01,500 --> 00:10:05,680
For example, if a woman is pregnant, she's making a baby, and each cell of the baby is

144
00:10:05,680 --> 00:10:09,080
going to have nuclear material, so folate would be necessary.

145
00:10:09,080 --> 00:10:14,000
If a patient has cancer, and cancer is generating cells that have nuclear material, folate would be

146
00:10:14,000 --> 00:10:15,540
necessary.

147
00:10:15,540 --> 00:10:20,520
And hemolytic anemia, where patients are rapidly turning over their bone marrow cells, would

148
00:10:20,520 --> 00:10:23,540
require an increased amount of folate.

149
00:10:23,540 --> 00:10:28,040
So increased demand can also cause a folate deficiency.

150
00:10:28,040 --> 00:10:33,620
And the final way, or a third way, I should say, by which folate deficiency can arise is

151
00:10:33,620 --> 00:10:35,380
via folate antagonists.

152
00:10:35,380 --> 00:10:41,740
Now, recall that methotrexate from pharmacology inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, that's important

153
00:10:41,740 --> 00:10:42,740
to know.

154
00:10:42,740 --> 00:10:49,060
Methotrexate inhibits dihydrofolate reductase, which can then result in a folate deficiency.

155
00:10:49,060 --> 00:10:53,840
And so these are some important causes of folate deficiency.

156
00:10:53,840 --> 00:10:58,180
When a patient becomes folate deficient, there are certain clinical and laboratory findings

157
00:10:58,180 --> 00:10:59,180
that would arise.

158
00:10:59,180 --> 00:11:05,060
Obviously, the patients would have a macrocytic anemia, i.e., the cells would have an MCV greater

159
00:11:05,060 --> 00:11:10,480
than 100, and we would see hypersegmented neutrophils on blood smear.

160
00:11:10,480 --> 00:11:15,880
In addition, the patients classically have glossitis, which is inflammation of the tongue,

161
00:11:15,880 --> 00:11:20,160
and that partially has to do with the fact that the cells of the tongue are not going

162
00:11:20,160 --> 00:11:26,220
to be turning over as well, and so there will be discomfort in the tongue.

163
00:11:26,220 --> 00:11:28,560
There will be a decreased serum folate.

164
00:11:28,560 --> 00:11:30,540
I don't think that's hard to understand.

165
00:11:30,540 --> 00:11:34,200
We're dealing with folate deficiency, and so in folate deficiency, the serum folate will

166
00:11:34,200 --> 00:11:35,340
be low.

167
00:11:35,340 --> 00:11:40,840
There will be an increased serum homocysteine, and this is very important.

168
00:11:40,840 --> 00:11:48,620
Recall that I told you that tetrahydrofolate gains a methyl group when it gets absorbed

169
00:11:48,620 --> 00:11:50,080
into the human body.

170
00:11:50,080 --> 00:11:58,280
It then passes that methyl group onto vitamin B12, and then vitamin B12 then passes that

171
00:11:58,280 --> 00:12:02,640
methyl group onto homocysteine.

172
00:12:02,640 --> 00:12:09,120
And so imagine that if there is no folate, then there is no passing of methyl to vitamin

173
00:12:09,120 --> 00:12:16,280
B12, then vitamin B12 cannot pass the methyl group to homocysteine to produce methionine,

174
00:12:16,280 --> 00:12:23,020
and so if homocysteine cannot become transferred, cannot be converted to methionine, then homocysteine

175
00:12:23,020 --> 00:12:29,100
is going to pile up or build up, and so there will be an increase in the serum homocysteine.

176
00:12:29,100 --> 00:12:33,600
And because this ties into biochemistry, and because it's useful clinically, it is important

177
00:12:33,600 --> 00:12:34,600
to know.

178
00:12:34,600 --> 00:12:39,460
The final clinical laboratory finding is actually a negative finding, but it's a helpful finding,

179
00:12:39,460 --> 00:12:43,380
and that is that the methylmalonic acid levels will be normal.

180
00:12:43,380 --> 00:12:49,480
Now methylmalonic acid gets converted to succinyl-CoA, and the mechanism by which that conversion

181
00:12:49,480 --> 00:12:53,400
occurs is via vitamin B12.

182
00:12:53,400 --> 00:13:01,060
Otherwise stated, vitamin B12 is necessary to convert methylmalonic acid to succinyl-CoA.

183
00:13:01,060 --> 00:13:05,860
Methylmalonic acid will be normal in these patients because this is not a vitamin B12 deficiency.

184
00:13:05,860 --> 00:13:07,880
We're discussing folate deficiency.

185
00:13:07,880 --> 00:13:12,300
But again, it's an important negative finding because when you have a megaloblastic anemia,

186
00:13:12,300 --> 00:13:17,680
the differential diagnosis would be folate or vitamin B12, and by seeing a normal methylmalonic

187
00:13:17,680 --> 00:13:23,140
acid, we would gain an understanding that the vitamin B12 levels are normal.

188
00:13:23,140 --> 00:13:28,440
Of course, we could also measure the vitamin B12 levels directly as well.

189
00:13:28,440 --> 00:13:34,420
That then brings us into a discussion of the second cause of megaloblastic anemia, and that

190
00:13:34,420 --> 00:13:37,200
is vitamin B12 deficiency.

191
00:13:37,200 --> 00:13:43,480
So let's discuss a little bit of the biochemistry of vitamin B12 in order to gain a better understanding

192
00:13:43,480 --> 00:13:50,480
of how a patient can become vitamin B12 deficient and hence develop a megaloblastic anemia.

193
00:13:50,480 --> 00:13:57,880
Vitamin B12 is complex to animal-derived proteins, for example, meat or eggs, and it comes into

194
00:13:57,880 --> 00:14:01,840
the body attached to those animal-derived proteins.

195
00:14:01,840 --> 00:14:09,220
Once it comes into the body, however, vitamin B12 will be cleaved from those animal proteins

196
00:14:09,220 --> 00:14:16,260
and will be bound by a protein called R binder.

197
00:14:16,260 --> 00:14:22,120
Now R binder is actually produced by the salivary gland, and so the salivary gland has the job

198
00:14:22,120 --> 00:14:26,180
of producing this protein that will then be complex to vitamin B12.

199
00:14:26,180 --> 00:14:33,180
R binder and vitamin B12 will then travel through the esophagus and travel through the stomach

200
00:14:33,180 --> 00:14:36,060
until eventually they reach the small bowel.

201
00:14:36,060 --> 00:14:41,420
When they reach the small bowel, R binder will be cleaved away from vitamin B12, and how

202
00:14:41,420 --> 00:14:43,060
is that going to occur?

203
00:14:43,060 --> 00:14:46,280
Via the proteases that are produced by the pancreas.

204
00:14:46,280 --> 00:14:53,560
Once vitamin B12 is cleaved away from R binder, vitamin B12 will then, in the intestine, bind intrinsic

205
00:14:53,560 --> 00:14:54,560
factor.

206
00:14:54,560 --> 00:15:00,520
An intrinsic factor is a protein that's produced by the parietal cells in the body of the stomach.

207
00:15:00,520 --> 00:15:05,440
Again intrinsic factor is a protein that's produced by the parietal cells in the body

208
00:15:05,440 --> 00:15:06,780
of the stomach.

209
00:15:06,780 --> 00:15:11,540
Once intrinsic factor binds vitamin B12, this complex will then go to the ileum and it will

210
00:15:11,540 --> 00:15:14,920
be absorbed in the ileum.

211
00:15:14,920 --> 00:15:21,500
This is the basic understanding of how vitamin B12 comes into the body.

212
00:15:21,500 --> 00:15:25,460
Vitamin B12 deficiency is less common than folate deficiency.

213
00:15:25,460 --> 00:15:29,460
And in addition, it takes years to develop a vitamin B12 deficiency.

214
00:15:29,460 --> 00:15:31,460
Why does it take years?

215
00:15:31,460 --> 00:15:33,380
Because there are large hepatic stores.

216
00:15:33,380 --> 00:15:39,760
The liver is loaded with vitamin B12, and so if a patient were to absolutely not see any

217
00:15:39,760 --> 00:15:45,260
animal protein, for example, a vegan, a very strict vegan who doesn't take milk, doesn't

218
00:15:45,260 --> 00:15:50,040
take eggs, etc., that individual could become vitamin B12 deficient.

219
00:15:50,300 --> 00:15:54,380
However, it would take years to develop due to large hepatic stores of vitamin B12.

220
00:15:54,380 --> 00:16:00,620
The most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency is pernicious anemia.

221
00:16:00,620 --> 00:16:06,200
Pernicious anemia is a disease that arises when there is autoimmune destruction of the parietal

222
00:16:06,200 --> 00:16:07,200
cells.

223
00:16:07,200 --> 00:16:11,460
Remind ourselves that the parietal cells are present in the body of the stomach and they

224
00:16:11,460 --> 00:16:13,760
produce intrinsic factor.

225
00:16:13,760 --> 00:16:19,060
Recall that intrinsic factor is necessary to bind vitamin B12 because the ileum recognizes

226
00:16:19,060 --> 00:16:25,520
the vitamin B12 intrinsic factor complex and that is what is absorbed by the ileum.

227
00:16:25,520 --> 00:16:29,840
So if there is a loss of the parietal cells, there will be a loss of intrinsic factor.

228
00:16:29,840 --> 00:16:35,260
And if there is a loss of intrinsic factor, there will be an inability to take up vitamin

229
00:16:35,260 --> 00:16:39,520
B12 and so the patients will become vitamin B12 deficient.

230
00:16:39,520 --> 00:16:43,560
And this happens to be the most common cause of vitamin B12 deficiency.

231
00:16:43,560 --> 00:16:48,420
I'd like to take a step back here and also remind ourselves a little bit about the parietal

232
00:16:48,420 --> 00:16:49,860
cells of the stomach.

233
00:16:49,860 --> 00:16:53,600
The way I think about the parietal cells of the stomach is that it's associated with

234
00:16:53,600 --> 00:16:54,880
a bunch of P's.

235
00:16:54,880 --> 00:16:59,640
So obviously parietal cell begins with the letter P, but in addition, the parietal cells

236
00:16:59,640 --> 00:17:02,280
are the ones that have the proton pumps.

237
00:17:02,280 --> 00:17:06,260
They are the ones that make the acid in the body of the stomach.

238
00:17:06,260 --> 00:17:09,780
In addition, the parietal cells are pink, so that's another P.

239
00:17:09,780 --> 00:17:10,940
And what do I mean by pink?

240
00:17:10,940 --> 00:17:14,760
When you look at the histology of the body of the stomach, you'll see chief cells and

241
00:17:14,760 --> 00:17:15,760
parietal cells.

242
00:17:15,760 --> 00:17:20,080
The chief cells look more blue and the parietal cells look more pink.

243
00:17:20,080 --> 00:17:25,080
And then the third P is that the parietal cells are associated with pernicious anemia.

244
00:17:25,080 --> 00:17:29,520
When you knock out the parietal cells due to autoimmune destruction, that results in

245
00:17:29,520 --> 00:17:31,560
pernicious anemia.

246
00:17:31,560 --> 00:17:36,000
There are some other causes of vitamin B deficiency and examiners like to go after these because

247
00:17:36,000 --> 00:17:42,200
they allow the examiners to test you on the biochemistry of vitamin B12 absorption.

248
00:17:42,200 --> 00:17:48,360
So, for example, pancreatic insufficiency could result in a vitamin B12 deficiency.

249
00:17:48,360 --> 00:17:49,520
Why would this happen?

250
00:17:49,520 --> 00:17:54,520
Because remember that the pancreas produces the enzymes necessary to cleave vitamin B12

251
00:17:54,520 --> 00:17:56,640
away from our binder.

252
00:17:56,640 --> 00:18:02,720
Go back and remind ourselves that vitamin B12 comes into the body, is then bound by our

253
00:18:02,720 --> 00:18:06,140
binder in the mouth, which is produced by the salivary glands.

254
00:18:06,140 --> 00:18:12,420
The R binder vitamin B12 complex then goes through the stomach and then goes into the small bowel.

255
00:18:12,420 --> 00:18:17,160
And it's in the small bowel that the pancreatic enzymes, particularly the proteases, will cleave

256
00:18:17,160 --> 00:18:22,040
away our binder, which will then allow intrinsic factor to bind vitamin B12.

257
00:18:22,040 --> 00:18:26,420
If there's pancreatic insufficiency and the enzymes are not produced properly, this could

258
00:18:26,420 --> 00:18:28,740
result in a vitamin B12 deficiency.

259
00:18:28,740 --> 00:18:34,620
So that would be one additional cause of vitamin B12 deficiency.

260
00:18:34,620 --> 00:18:38,960
Another cause of vitamin B12 deficiency would be damage to the terminal ileum.

261
00:18:38,960 --> 00:18:43,500
Remember that vitamin B12 is absorbed in the ileum.

262
00:18:43,500 --> 00:18:47,720
And so if there's damage, for example, to the terminal ileum, as would be seen in Crohn's

263
00:18:47,720 --> 00:18:55,620
disease or as would be seen with fish tapeworm, then that would result in a vitamin B12 deficiency.

264
00:18:55,620 --> 00:18:59,740
A third cause that I've listed here is dietary deficiency.

265
00:18:59,740 --> 00:19:04,060
Recall, and I would like to really hit home on this point because, again, examiners like

266
00:19:04,060 --> 00:19:09,300
to go after it, it's very rare to see dietary deficiency except in vegans.

267
00:19:09,300 --> 00:19:10,300
And what are vegans?

268
00:19:10,300 --> 00:19:15,660
These are strict vegetarians that do not eat anything that comes from an animal.

269
00:19:15,660 --> 00:19:19,000
So for example, they will not eat an egg.

270
00:19:19,000 --> 00:19:22,880
That could then lead to a dietary deficiency.

271
00:19:22,880 --> 00:19:28,260
The clinical and laboratory findings of vitamin B12 deficiency are much of what we've already

272
00:19:28,260 --> 00:19:29,260
covered.

273
00:19:29,260 --> 00:19:34,980
So for example, there will be a macrocytic anemia with an MCV greater than 100 with hypersegmented

274
00:19:34,980 --> 00:19:35,980
neutrophils.

275
00:19:35,980 --> 00:19:43,480
There will also be glossitis due to poor turnover of the cells of the surface of the tongue.

276
00:19:43,480 --> 00:19:48,560
And third, and very important in the context of this discussion, is that the patients can

277
00:19:48,560 --> 00:19:52,940
get a subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord.

278
00:19:52,940 --> 00:19:57,480
Recall from biochemistry that there are only two chemical reactions that vitamin B12 is

279
00:19:57,480 --> 00:19:58,880
involved in.

280
00:19:58,880 --> 00:20:03,040
One is the reaction that involves a synthesis of DNA precursors.

281
00:20:03,040 --> 00:20:06,780
Remember that folate transfers its methyl group to vitamin B12.

282
00:20:06,780 --> 00:20:11,620
The second is the conversion of methylmalonic acid to succinyl-CoA.

283
00:20:11,620 --> 00:20:14,620
This reaction requires vitamin B12.

284
00:20:14,620 --> 00:20:21,440
If vitamin B12 is lacking, methylmalonic acid cannot be converted to succinyl-CoA and methylmalonic

285
00:20:21,440 --> 00:20:29,180
acid will then subsequently build up, in particular, within the myelin of the spinal cord.

286
00:20:29,180 --> 00:20:35,540
And as it builds up within the myelin of the spinal cord, it will cause degeneration of that,

287
00:20:35,540 --> 00:20:38,020
of certain regions of the spinal cord.

288
00:20:38,020 --> 00:20:42,020
And that's called subacute combined degeneration of the spinal cord.

289
00:20:42,020 --> 00:20:47,000
It's very important to remember that vitamin B12 is associated with this neurologic finding,

290
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:51,960
and that this finding would not be seen in folate deficiency.

291
00:20:51,960 --> 00:20:56,420
Laboratory findings would include a decrease in serum vitamin B12.

292
00:20:56,420 --> 00:21:01,000
In addition, there would be an increase in serum homocysteine, same concept.

293
00:21:01,000 --> 00:21:07,740
If you have tetrahydrofolate, which is folate that comes into the body, it quickly gets methylated.

294
00:21:07,740 --> 00:21:13,740
After that methyl group gets attached to vitamin B12, and then vitamin B12 eventually takes that

295
00:21:13,740 --> 00:21:16,740
methyl group and passes it on to homocysteine.

296
00:21:16,740 --> 00:21:21,740
And homocysteine will then get converted to methionine.

297
00:21:21,740 --> 00:21:28,740
So if vitamin B12 is not present, it will not be able to pass this methyl group to homocysteine,

298
00:21:28,740 --> 00:21:31,740
and so homocysteine levels will rise.

299
00:21:31,740 --> 00:21:34,740
And so the serum homocysteine will be high.

300
00:21:34,740 --> 00:21:39,740
The third lab finding would be an increase in methylmalonic acid.

301
00:21:39,740 --> 00:21:48,740
And I've already explained that vitamin B12 is necessary to convert methylmalonic acid to succinyl-CoA.

302
00:21:48,740 --> 00:21:54,740
And, of course, that methylmalonic acid, as it builds up, will eventually damage the spinal cord.

303
00:21:54,740 --> 00:21:57,740
And that concludes our discussion of macrocytic anemia.

304
00:21:57,740 --> 00:21:58,740
And that concludes our discussion of macrocytic anemia.

